{
  "title": "Where are you going? — on foot or by vehicle",
  "title_target": "Куди ти йдеш? — Kudy ty ydesh?",
  "goal": "Say where you're going, choose between two different verbs for \"go\", and keep place (де) and direction (куди) firmly apart: the pair beginners mix up most.",
  "bekal": [
    "tell йти (on foot) from їхати (by vehicle)",
    "answer Куди? with в/на + знахідний (accusative)",
    "use Де? and Куди? in the same breath",
    "name transport with на + місцевий (locative)",
    "invite someone with Ходімо!"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Діалог — At the metro entrance",
      "intro": "Ani and Andriy run into each other in the street. Watch the two verbs that both mean \"go\": Ani is walking, Andriy is riding, and Ukrainian won't let them use the same word.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Ані|A-nee], [куди|koo-DI] [ти|ti] [йдеш|y-DESH]?",
          "id": "Ani, where are you going?",
          "literal": "Ani, where-to — you — walk?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Я|Ya] [йду|y-DOO] [на|na] [пошту|POSH-too]. [А|A] [потім|PO-teem] [у|oo] [бібліотеку|beeb-lee-o-TEH-koo].",
          "id": "I'm going to the post office. Then to the library.",
          "literal": "I — walk — to — post-office (accusative). and — then — to — library (accusative)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Пішки|PEESH-ki]? [Це|Tseh] [далеко|da-LEH-ko].",
          "id": "On foot? That's far.",
          "literal": "on-foot? this — far."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Не|Neh] [дуже|DOO-zheh]. [Двадцять|DVAD-tsyat'] [хвилин|khvi-LIN].",
          "id": "Not very. Twenty minutes.",
          "literal": "not — very. twenty — minutes."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Краще|KRA-shcheh] [їдь|yeed'] [на|na] [автобусі|av-TO-boo-see]. [Номер|NO-mer] [сім|seem].",
          "id": "Better take the bus. Number seven.",
          "literal": "better — ride — on — bus (locative). number — seven."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[А|A] [ти|ti] [куди|koo-DI] [їдеш|YEE-desh]?",
          "id": "And where are you going?",
          "literal": "and — you — where-to — ride?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Я|Ya] [їду|YEE-doo] [на|na] [роботу|ro-BO-too]. [У|OO] [кафе|ka-FEH].",
          "id": "I'm going to work. To the café.",
          "literal": "I — ride — to — work (accusative). to — café."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[На|Na] [метро|met-RO]?",
          "id": "By metro?",
          "literal": "on — metro?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Так|Tak]. [Це|Tseh] [швидше|SHVID-sheh].",
          "id": "Yes. It's faster.",
          "literal": "yes. this — faster."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Тоді|To-DEE] [ходімо|kho-DEE-mo] [разом|RA-zom] [до|do] [метро|met-RO].",
          "id": "Then let's walk to the metro together.",
          "literal": "then — let's-walk — together — up-to — metro."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Добре|DOB-reh]. [А|A] [завтра|ZAV-tra] [ти|ti] [йдеш|y-DESH] [на|na] [концерт|kon-TSERT]?",
          "id": "OK. Are you going to the concert tomorrow?",
          "literal": "good. and — tomorrow — you — walk — to — concert?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Обов'язково|O-bo-VYAZ-ko-vo]! [Я|Ya] [вже|vzheh] [купила|koo-PI-la] [квиток|kvi-TOK].",
          "id": "Definitely! I've already bought a ticket.",
          "literal": "definitely! I — already — bought (past f) — ticket."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Йти for going on foot, їхати for going by vehicle.** Ukrainian has no neutral verb for \"go\". If you say *йду* when you're taking the bus, you'll still be understood, but it sounds as if you're walking to Lviv. ⚠️ English speakers slip here most, because English *go* covers both.",
        "**Куди takes the accusative, Де takes the locative, with exactly the same preposition.** *Я йду в університет* (direction) versus *Я в університеті* (place). The preposition is *в* in both; the ending tells the story. This is the most important pair in the whole book.",
        "**Transport takes на + locative.** *На автобусі*, *на метро*, *на потязі*, *на таксі*, *на машині*. Notice that this collides with the direction rule: *їду на роботу* (direction, accusative) but *їду на автобусі* (means, locative). Context keeps them apart, and your ear will adjust quickly.",
        "**Пішки stands alone, with no preposition.** *Я йду пішки*: I'm walking, I'm going on foot. It's the only way to say \"on foot\", and it's an adverb, not a noun.",
        "**Ходімо! = let's go (on foot).** A very common and very useful way to invite someone. For vehicles: **поїхали!** (let's go, off we go). You'll hear both often from Ukrainian friends, and both mean \"let's go\".",
        "**Пошта takes на, not в.** *На пошту* (to the post office), *на пошті* (at the post office). It belongs to the same group as *на роботу*, *на вокзал*, *на ринок*, *на концерт*, *на урок*. The group is small, but it's full of places you visit often, so learn them together.",
        "**До метро, not в метро.** The preposition **до** means \"up to\": you walk as far as the entrance, not inside. Compare *йду до метро* (heading to the station) with *їду на метро* (riding the train)."
      ],
